+// ZyperOS: two buffers and a lock. With the OpenGL backends the PSP's sound
+// is mixed on the emulation thread (System_AudioPushSamples, 64 frames every
+// 1.45 ms), while retro_run hands the buffer to the frontend on the libretro
+// thread and then empties it. Nothing kept the two apart, so the blocks the
+// emulation thread pushed while the frontend was taking the buffer were wiped
+// by the reset after it, and a realloc there could free the buffer under the
+// reader. Each lost block is a 1.45 ms gap: the PSP's sound crackled. Now the
+// emulation thread fills one buffer under the lock, and retro_run swaps it
+// for the drained one under the lock and hands it over outside it, so neither
+// thread waits on the other for longer than a swap. (PPSSPP fixed the race
+// after v1.20.4, in 00f5f4fc90, by holding its lock across the frontend's
+// callback; this does not stall the emulation thread on the frontend.)
+static AudioBuffer output_audio_buffer = {NULL, 0, 0};    // the emulation thread's
+static AudioBuffer drained_audio_buffer = {NULL, 0, 0};   // retro_run's
+static std::mutex output_audio_buffer_mutex;
 
